  "id": "Compound::DB01165 - Gene::6584",
  "verbalization": "Ofloxacin, a quinolone antibiotic, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Candiduria through a mechanism involving the inhibition of bacterial growth, which is supported by its interaction with Norfloxacin and Lomefloxacin, both quinolones. These antibiotics bind to Slc22a5, a transporter protein, suggesting a potential role in the pathophysiology of Candiduria. The shared pathway between Ofloxacin and Norfloxacin, as well as Lomefloxacin, to Slc22a5, indicates that the interaction is not unique to quinolones but is a broader property of quinolone antibiotics. The evidence suggests that the binding of quinolones to Slc22a5 may contribute to their pathogenic effects, particularly in the context of Candiduria. The recurring involvement of quinolones in this relationship underscores the importance of targeting this transporter in the development of novel antimicrobial strategies.",
